# Files copied into every worktree Claude Code creates with git.
#
# A worktree is a fresh checkout, so gitignored local config is absent and the
# first build in a new worktree fails for reasons unrelated to the ticket.
# Syntax is .gitignore syntax. Only files that match AND are gitignored are
# copied, so tracked files (.env.example) are never duplicated.
#
# Not processed if a WorktreeCreate hook replaces the default git logic.
# Reference: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/worktrees

.env
.env.*
*.local.md
*.local.mdx

# The working-documentation tree, so a session in a worktree can reach it
# rather than working without it. What it holds is not described here, for the
# reason .gitignore gives beside the same rule.
#
# THE RULE SHAPE IS LOAD-BEARING, and this is measured rather than reasoned.
# The copier enumerates gitignored entries the way `git status --ignored` does,
# which COLLAPSES a wholly-ignored directory to its own name. So:
#
#   a FILE glob       does not reach a file inside such a directory: the file
#                     was never an entry to match, only the directory was
#   a DIRECTORY rule  copies the whole tree, matching that collapsed entry
#
# Verified in a fresh worktree rather than inferred: the tree arrives with this
# line and does not without it. Do not "simplify" this to a glob; it would keep
# looking like a rule for the same thing while delivering nothing.
#
# TREAT THE COPY AS READ-ONLY. It is a one-way snapshot, not a link. The tree is
# gitignored, so an edit made inside a worktree reaches neither the main
# checkout nor git, and caail-pr-wrapup's cleanup deletes the worktree with the
# edit still in it. Two parallel worktrees each get an independent snapshot with
# no merge path. Write to the main checkout.
# WHAT THIS WIDENS, stated because it is a deliberate trade rather than an
# oversight. This rule copies the tree into EVERY worktree the harness creates,
# which includes the short-lived ones spawned for subagents, not only the ones a
# person opens. Before it, a subagent worktree had no copy and could not read the
# tree at all. The publishing rule notes that subagents cross trust boundaries
# silently, and the publish guard covers `gh issue/pr/release/gist` only, so a
# commit message or a branch name is not on that path. The trade was made because
# a worktree session could otherwise not reach the working documentation at all,
# and worktrees are this repo's normal working shape.
/internal-docs/
